## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a compact, eviction-aware data structure for time-windowed counting, a common pattern in real-time analytics.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window eviction via timestamped events
- Bucketed counts: map from count to set of users for fast top-k
- Invariants: update counts atomically during insertion and eviction
- Complexity: O(log n) updates, O(k log n) top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.count = new Map(); // user -> cnt
    this.buckets = new Map(); // cnt -> Set<user>
    this.events = new Deque(); // (ts, user)
  }
  _addToBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) this.buckets.set(cnt, new Set());
    this.buckets.get(cnt).add(user);
  }
  _removeFromBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) return;
    this.buckets.get(cnt).delete(user);
    if (this.buckets.get(cnt).size === 0) this.buckets.delete(cnt);
  }
  prune(now) {
    while (this.events.length && this.events.peek().ts <= now - this.W) {
      const {ts, user} = this.events.shift();
      const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
      if (c > 0) {
        this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
        const nc = c - 1;
        if (nc > 0) {
          this._addToBucket(nc, user);
          this.count.set(user, nc);
        } else {
          this.count.delete(user);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  addEvent(user, ts) {
    this.events.push({ts, user});
    this.prune(ts);
    const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
    if (c > 0) this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
    const nc = c + 1;
    this._addToBucket(nc, user);
    this.count.set(user, nc);
  }
  topK(now, k) {
    const res = [];
    // iterate counts from high to low
    const counts = Array.from(this.buckets.keys()).sort((a,b)=>b-a);
    for (const cnt of counts) {
      const set = this.buckets.get(cnt);
      const users = Array.from(set).sort((a,b)=>a-b); // tie-breaker by userId
      for (const u of users) {
        res.push([u, cnt]);
        if (res.length === k) return res;
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support multiple simultaneous windows (e.g., W1 and W2) efficiently?
- How would you handle very large userId spaces with memory constraints, or concurrent updates in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of order statistics in dynamic sets, along with practical implementation details like lazy deletion and handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts

- **Median of a dynamic set**: the middle element (or average of two middle elements) when sorted
- **Two-heap approach**: max-heap for the lower half, min-heap for the upper half
- **Lazy deletion**: defer actual removal until top operations, tracking deletions via a count map
- **Duplicate handling**: maintain frequency counts rather than storing duplicate values multiple times in the heaps

## Code Example

```javascript
class MedianFinder {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();    // max-heap for lower half
    this.high = new MinHeap();   // min-heap for upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();    // lazy deletion map
  }

  insert(num) {
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(num);
    } else {
      this.high.push(num);
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  rebalance() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  erase(num) {
    this.delayed.set(num, (this.delayed.get(num) || 0) + 1);
    if (num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.deleted++;
    } else {
      this.high.deleted++;
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  getMedian() {
    this.prune(this.low);
    this.prune(this.high);
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) {
      return this.low.peek();
    }
    return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

